IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `wra-core`

-- Welfare Reform Act 2012 (United Kingdom), 2012 c. 5, Part 5, ss 117 to 119: loss of benefit after a benefit offence.
-- Layer 1, part wra4. Host Act named in labels: SSFA (Social Security Fraud Act 2001), whose sections 6A, 6B, 7 and 11 these
-- sections amend. SSFA 6B(1) to (13) and 7(2) to (5) are not among the sources: what they say is a borrowed fact, asked as an input.
-- Result convention: JUST FALSE / FALSE = the text says no; NOTHING = not covered (see wra4-ss96-97.l4).
-- Seam 7 (PLAN section 8): the lists of "disqualifying benefit" and "sanctionable benefit" are completed by another part's limb
-- (Schedule 2 adds universal credit), so here every benefit this part does not name is NOTHING (list not closed), never FALSE.
-- Periods are counted with both the first and the last day included (the keel's Period); see the fork register.

§ `s 117 -- disqualifying and sanctionable benefits`

-- SSFA 6A(1) "disqualifying benefit" gains (f) child tax credit and (g) working tax credit.
-- src:1959 | (2) In subsection (1), in the definition of “disqualifying benefit”, after paragraph (e) there is inserted—
-- src:1960 | (f) child tax credit;
-- src:1961 | (g) working tax credit;
@ref s 117(2) SSFA 6A(1)(f)-(g) (src:1959-1961)
GIVEN `the benefit` IS A Benefit  @desc Which benefit is in question?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 117(2) SSFA 6A(1) the benefit is a disqualifying benefit` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the benefit`
    WHEN `child tax credit` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `working tax credit` THEN JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING                                     -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): this subsection inserts only (f) and (g) into a definition whose other paragraphs are not printed

-- SSFA 6A(1) "sanctionable benefit" gains (fa) child tax credit and (fb) working tax credit.
-- src:1962 | (3) In that subsection, in the definition of “sanctionable benefit”, after paragraph (f) there is inserted—
-- src:1963 | (fa) child tax credit;
-- src:1964 | (fb) working tax credit;
@ref s 117(3) SSFA 6A(1)(fa)-(fb) (src:1962-1964)
GIVEN `the benefit` IS A Benefit  @desc Which benefit is in question?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 117(3) SSFA 6A(1) the benefit is a sanctionable benefit` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the benefit`
    WHEN `child tax credit` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `working tax credit` THEN JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING                                     -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): this subsection inserts only (fa) and (fb) into a definition whose other paragraphs are not printed

§ `s 118 -- the period of the sanction after a conviction, penalty or caution`

-- SSFA 6B(1) is not printed. 6B(11A) names its paragraphs: a case within (1)(a), a case within (1)(b) or (c).
DECLARE `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) case within subsection (1)` IS ONE OF
    `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) a case within subsection (1)(a)`
    `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) a case within subsection (1)(b)`
    `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) a case within subsection (1)(c)`
    `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) a case within none of paragraphs (a) to (c)`

DECLARE `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) relevant period` IS ONE OF
    `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) the period of three years`
    `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) the period of 13 weeks`
    `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) the period of four weeks`

-- 6B(14): "relevant offence". The record gathers what the definition looks at for one offence on conviction.
DECLARE `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(ii) kind of sentence` IS ONE OF
    `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(ii) a custodial sentence`
    `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(ii) a suspended sentence as defined in section 189(7)(b) of the Criminal Justice Act 2003`
    `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(ii) some other sentence`

DECLARE `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14) conviction for a benefit offence` HAS
    `the place where the proceedings were brought`                           IS A Place
    `the offence is the common law offence of conspiracy to defraud`         IS A BOOLEAN
    `the offence is a prescribed offence`                                    IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
    `the offence was committed in the circumstances regulations prescribe`   IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
    `the overpayment the court found the offence to relate to, in pounds`    IS A MAYBE NUMBER
    `the sentence imposed`                                                   IS A `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(ii) kind of sentence`
    `the length of the sentence imposed, in months`                          IS A NUMBER
    `the period the court found the offence to have been committed over, in months` IS A MAYBE NUMBER

-- 6B(14)(a): "in England and Wales, the common law offence of conspiracy to defraud". The place is where the proceedings
-- were brought (fork register): England and Wales are Greater London, England outside Greater London, and Wales.
-- src:1975 | (14) In this section and section 7 “relevant offence” means—
-- src:1976 | (a) in England and Wales, the common law offence of conspiracy to defraud, or
@ref s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(a) (src:1975-1976)
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14) conviction for a benefit offence`  @desc Which conviction for a benefit offence is in question?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(a) the offence is the common law offence of conspiracy to defraud in England and Wales` MEANS
    IF `the conviction`'s `the offence is the common law offence of conspiracy to defraud`
    THEN (CONSIDER `the conviction`'s `the place where the proceedings were brought`
          WHEN `Greater London` THEN TRUE
          WHEN `England outside Greater London` THEN TRUE
          WHEN Wales THEN TRUE
          WHEN Scotland THEN FALSE                        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(a) in England and Wales, the common law offence of conspiracy to defraud"
          WHEN `Northern Ireland` THEN FALSE              -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(a) in England and Wales, the common law offence of conspiracy to defraud"
          WHEN `outside the United Kingdom` THEN FALSE)   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(a) in England and Wales, the common law offence of conspiracy to defraud"
    ELSE FALSE                                            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(a) in England and Wales, the common law offence of conspiracy to defraud"

-- 6B(14)(b)(i): the court finds the offence to relate to an overpayment of at least 50,000 pounds. No finding is no such finding.
-- src:1978 | (i) is found by the court to relate to an overpayment (as defined in section 115A(8) of the Administration Act) of at least £50,000,
@ref s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(i) (src:1978-1978)
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14) conviction for a benefit offence`  @desc Which conviction for a benefit offence is in question?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(i) the court found an overpayment of at least 50,000 pounds` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the conviction`'s `the overpayment the court found the offence to relate to, in pounds`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE                               -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(i) is found by the court to relate to an overpayment" (no such finding)
    WHEN JUST amount THEN amount AT LEAST 50000

-- 6B(14)(b)(ii): punished by a custodial sentence of at least one year, a suspended sentence included. One year is read as 12 months.
-- src:1979 | (ii) is punished by a custodial sentence of at least one year (including a suspended sentence as defined in section 189(7)(b) of the Criminal Justice Act 2003), or
@ref s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(ii) (src:1979-1979)
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14) conviction for a benefit offence`  @desc Which conviction for a benefit offence is in question?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(ii) the sentence is a custodial sentence of at least one year` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the conviction`'s `the sentence imposed`
    WHEN `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(ii) a custodial sentence` THEN `the conviction`'s `the length of the sentence imposed, in months` AT LEAST 12
    WHEN `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(ii) a suspended sentence as defined in section 189(7)(b) of the Criminal Justice Act 2003` THEN `the conviction`'s `the length of the sentence imposed, in months` AT LEAST 12
    WHEN `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(ii) some other sentence` THEN FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(ii) is punished by a custodial sentence of at least one year (including a suspended sentence ...)"

-- 6B(14)(b)(iii): the court finds the offence to have been committed over a period of at least two years (24 months).
-- src:1980 | (iii) is found by the court to have been committed over a period of at least two years.
@ref s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(iii) (src:1980-1980)
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14) conviction for a benefit offence`  @desc Which conviction for a benefit offence is in question?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(iii) the court found the offence to have been committed over at least two years` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the conviction`'s `the period the court found the offence to have been committed over, in months`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE                               -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(iii) is found by the court to have been committed over a period of at least two years" (no such finding)
    WHEN JUST months THEN months AT LEAST 24

-- 6B(14)(b): a prescribed offence committed in prescribed circumstances which, on conviction, meets (i), (ii) or (iii).
-- What is prescribed is left to regulations, so an unanswered question about it is NOTHING (undefined term).
-- src:1977 | (b) a prescribed offence which, in the offender's case, is committed in such circumstances as may be prescribed, and which, on conviction—
@ref s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b) (src:1977-1977)
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14) conviction for a benefit offence`  @desc Which conviction for a benefit offence is in question?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b) the offence is a prescribed offence of the kind that is a relevant offence` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the conviction`'s `the offence is a prescribed offence`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "a prescribed offence" is whatever regulations prescribe
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE                       -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(b) a prescribed offence which, in the offender's case, is committed in such circumstances as may be prescribed"
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
        CONSIDER `the conviction`'s `the offence was committed in the circumstances regulations prescribe`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                         -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "such circumstances as may be prescribed" are whatever regulations prescribe
        WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE                   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "which, in the offender's case, is committed in such circumstances as may be prescribed"
        WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
            IF `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(i) the court found an overpayment of at least 50,000 pounds` `the conviction`
               OR `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(ii) the sentence is a custodial sentence of at least one year` `the conviction`
               OR `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b)(iii) the court found the offence to have been committed over at least two years` `the conviction`
            THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE JUST FALSE                               -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "and which, on conviction— (i) ... (ii) ... or (iii) ..."

-- 6B(14): "relevant offence" means (a) or (b). A closed definition ("means").
-- src:1975 | (14) In this section and section 7 “relevant offence” means—
@ref s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14) (src:1975-1975)
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14) conviction for a benefit offence`  @desc Which conviction for a benefit offence is in question?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14) the offence is a relevant offence` MEANS
    IF `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(a) the offence is the common law offence of conspiracy to defraud in England and Wales` `the conviction`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE `s 118(5) SSFA 6B(14)(b) the offence is a prescribed offence of the kind that is a relevant offence` `the conviction`

-- 6B(11A): the relevant period. (a) in a case within (1)(a) where the benefit offence, or one of them, is a relevant offence:
-- three years; (b) in a case within (1)(a) but not within (a): 13 weeks; (c) in a case within (1)(b) or (c): four weeks.
-- src:1970 | (11A) For the purposes of subsection (11) the relevant period is—
-- src:1971 | (a) in a case falling within subsection (1)(a) where the benefit offence, or one of them, is a relevant offence, the period of three years,
-- src:1972 | (b) in a case falling within subsection (1)(a) (but not within paragraph (a) above)), the period of 13 weeks, or
-- src:1973 | (c) in a case falling within subsection (1)(b) or (c), the period of four weeks.
@ref s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) (src:1970-1973)
GIVEN `the case` IS A `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) case within subsection (1)`  @desc Is the sanction one that follows a conviction (paragraph (a) of section 6B(1)), a penalty (paragraph (b)) or a caution (paragraph (c)), or none of these?
      `the benefit offence, or one of them, is a relevant offence` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Is the benefit offence, or one of them, a "relevant offence" (see section 118(5))? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) relevant period`
`s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) the relevant period` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the case`
    WHEN `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) a case within subsection (1)(a)` THEN
        CONSIDER `the benefit offence, or one of them, is a relevant offence`
        WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) the period of three years`
        WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) the period of 13 weeks`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                         -- NOTHING-kind (silent): whether the offence is a relevant offence is not known, and the two paragraphs give different periods
    WHEN `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) a case within subsection (1)(b)` THEN JUST `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) the period of four weeks`
    WHEN `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) a case within subsection (1)(c)` THEN JUST `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) the period of four weeks`
    WHEN `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) a case within none of paragraphs (a) to (c)` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection gives a period only for the three cases it names

-- The last day of the period beginning on a given day, both days counting: three years is the day before the third
-- anniversary; 13 weeks is 91 days; four weeks is 28 days.
GIVEN `the period` IS A `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) relevant period`  @desc Which period applies to you?
      `the first day of the period` IS A DATE  @desc On what day does the period begin?
GIVETH A DATE
`s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) the last day of the relevant period` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the period`
    WHEN `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) the period of three years` THEN DATE_FROM_SERIAL ((DATE_SERIAL (`add years` `the first day of the period` 3)) MINUS 1)
    WHEN `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) the period of 13 weeks` THEN DATE_FROM_SERIAL ((DATE_SERIAL `the first day of the period`) PLUS 90)
    WHEN `s 118(4) SSFA 6B(11A) the period of four weeks` THEN DATE_FROM_SERIAL ((DATE_SERIAL `the first day of the period`) PLUS 27)

-- 7(1A): the restrictions in section 7 do not apply if the benefit offence, or any of them, is a relevant offence.
-- src:1984 | (1A) The following restrictions do not apply if the benefit offence referred to in subsection (1)(a), or any of them, is a relevant offence.
@ref s 118(7) SSFA 7(1A) (src:1984-1984)
GIVEN `the benefit offence referred to in section 7(1)(a), or any of them, is a relevant offence` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Is the benefit offence, or any of them, a "relevant offence" (see section 118(5))? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 118(7) SSFA 7(1A) the restrictions in section 7 do not apply` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the benefit offence referred to in section 7(1)(a), or any of them, is a relevant offence`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "The following restrictions do not apply if ... a relevant offence"; the subsection does not say they apply otherwise
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (silent): whether the offence is a relevant offence is not known

§ `s 119 -- sanctions for repeated benefit fraud`

-- SSFA 7(1)(b) and 7(6A) count five years back from a date. A date is "within the period of five years ending on" a day
-- when it is on or before that day and after the day five years earlier (fork register).
GIVEN dates IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 119(5) SSFA 7(1)(b) the most recent of the dates` MEANS
    CONSIDER dates
    WHEN EMPTY THEN NOTHING                               -- NOTHING-kind (silent): an empty list has no most recent date
    WHEN first FOLLOWED BY others THEN
        CONSIDER `s 119(5) SSFA 7(1)(b) the most recent of the dates` others
        WHEN NOTHING THEN JUST first
        WHEN JUST later THEN
            IF later GREATER THAN first
            THEN JUST later
            ELSE JUST first

-- 7(1)(b): within the five years ending on the date the benefit offence was, or any of them were, committed, one or more
-- disqualifying events occurred; the event, or the most recent of them, is "the earlier disqualifying event".
-- src:1997 | (b) within the period of five years ending on the date on which the benefit offence was, or any of them were, committed, one or more disqualifying events occurred in relation to the offender (the event, or the most recent of them, being referred to in this section as “the earlier disqualifying event”),
@ref s 119(5) SSFA 7(1)(b) (src:1997-1997)
GIVEN `the dates on which the benefit offences were committed` IS A LIST OF DATE  @desc On what date was each benefit offence committed?
      `the dates on which disqualifying events occurred in relation to the offender` IS A LIST OF DATE  @desc On what date did each disqualifying event happen in relation to the offender?
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 119(5) SSFA 7(1)(b) the earlier disqualifying event` MEANS
    `s 119(5) SSFA 7(1)(b) the most recent of the dates`
        (filter (GIVEN event YIELD any (GIVEN committed YIELD (event AT MOST committed) AND (event GREATER THAN (`add years` committed (0 MINUS 5)))) `the dates on which the benefit offences were committed`)
                `the dates on which disqualifying events occurred in relation to the offender`)

@ref s 119(5) SSFA 7(1)(b) (src:1997-1997)
GIVEN `the dates on which the benefit offences were committed` IS A LIST OF DATE  @desc On what date was each benefit offence committed?
      `the dates on which disqualifying events occurred in relation to the offender` IS A LIST OF DATE  @desc On what date did each disqualifying event happen in relation to the offender?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 119(5) SSFA 7(1)(b) a disqualifying event occurred within five years before a benefit offence` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 119(5) SSFA 7(1)(b) the earlier disqualifying event` `the dates on which the benefit offences were committed` `the dates on which disqualifying events occurred in relation to the offender`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE                               -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(b) within the period of five years ending on the date on which the benefit offence was, or any of them were, committed, one or more disqualifying events occurred" (none did)
    WHEN JUST anEvent THEN TRUE

-- 7(1)(a) to (e): the conditions for the section's restrictions. The words that introduce them in 7(1) are not printed, so
-- the function says only whether the five conditions are all met. (a), (c), (d) and (e) are facts asked as they stand.
-- src:1996 | (a) a person (“the offender”) is convicted of one or more benefit offences in a set of proceedings (“the current set of proceedings”),
-- src:1998 | (c) the current set of proceedings has not been taken into account for the purposes of any previous application of this section or section 8 or 9 in relation to the offender or any person who was then a member of his family,
-- src:1999 | (d) the earlier disqualifying event has not been taken into account as an earlier disqualifying event for the purposes of any previous application of this section or either of those sections in relation to the offender or any person who was then a member of his family, and
-- src:2000 | (e) the offender is a person with respect to whom the conditions for an entitlement to a sanctionable benefit are or become satisfied at any time within the disqualification period,
@ref s 119(5) SSFA 7(1)(a)-(e) (src:1996-2000)
GIVEN `the offender is convicted of one or more benefit offences in a set of proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Are you convicted of one or more benefit offences in a set of proceedings?
      `the dates on which the benefit offences were committed` IS A LIST OF DATE  @desc On what date was each benefit offence committed?
      `the dates on which disqualifying events occurred in relation to the offender` IS A LIST OF DATE  @desc On what date did each disqualifying event happen in relation to you?
      `the current set of proceedings has not been taken into account for any previous application of this section or section 8 or 9` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has this set of proceedings been left out of every earlier application of these sections to you or to anyone who was then a member of your family?
      `the earlier disqualifying event has not been taken into account for any previous application of this section or section 8 or 9` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has the earlier disqualifying event been left out of every earlier application of these sections to you or to anyone who was then a member of your family?
      `the conditions for an entitlement to a sanctionable benefit are or become satisfied within the disqualification period` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Are the conditions for an entitlement to a sanctionable benefit met for you, or do they become met, at any time within the disqualification period?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 119(5) SSFA 7(1) the conditions in paragraphs (a) to (e) are met` MEANS
    IF `the offender is convicted of one or more benefit offences in a set of proceedings`
       AND `s 119(5) SSFA 7(1)(b) a disqualifying event occurred within five years before a benefit offence` `the dates on which the benefit offences were committed` `the dates on which disqualifying events occurred in relation to the offender`
       AND `the current set of proceedings has not been taken into account for any previous application of this section or section 8 or 9`
       AND `the earlier disqualifying event has not been taken into account for any previous application of this section or section 8 or 9`
       AND `the conditions for an entitlement to a sanctionable benefit are or become satisfied within the disqualification period`
    THEN TRUE
    ELSE FALSE                                            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(a) ..., (b) ..., (c) ..., (d) ..., and (e) ..." (one of the five is not met)

-- 7(6A): the relevant period is (a) three years where, within the five years ending on the day the earlier disqualifying
-- event occurred, a previous one occurred; (b) in any other case, 26 weeks. 7(8A)(c) and (d) let a set of proceedings with
-- several convictions count on the date of any of its convictions, so the dates are lists.
DECLARE `s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) relevant period` IS ONE OF
    `s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) the period of three years`
    `s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) the period of 26 weeks`

-- src:2003 | (6A) For the purposes of subsection (6) the relevant period is—
-- src:2004 | (a) in a case where, within the period of five years ending on the date on which the earlier disqualifying event occurred, a previous disqualifying event occurred in relation to the offender, the period of three years;
-- src:2005 | (b) in any other case, 26 weeks.
@ref s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) (src:2003-2005)
GIVEN `the dates on which the earlier disqualifying event is taken to have occurred` IS A LIST OF DATE  @desc On what date did the earlier disqualifying event happen (for a set of proceedings with several convictions, the date of each conviction)?
      `the dates on which a previous disqualifying event is taken to have occurred` IS A LIST OF DATE  @desc On what date did a previous disqualifying event happen (for a set of proceedings with several convictions, the date of each conviction)?
GIVETH A `s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) relevant period`
`s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) the relevant period` MEANS
    IF any (GIVEN earlier YIELD any (GIVEN previous YIELD (previous AT MOST earlier) AND (previous GREATER THAN (`add years` earlier (0 MINUS 5)))) `the dates on which a previous disqualifying event is taken to have occurred`) `the dates on which the earlier disqualifying event is taken to have occurred`
    THEN `s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) the period of three years`
    ELSE `s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) the period of 26 weeks`

GIVEN `the period` IS A `s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) relevant period`  @desc Which period applies to you?
      `the first day of the period` IS A DATE  @desc On what day does the period begin?
GIVETH A DATE
`s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) the last day of the relevant period` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the period`
    WHEN `s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) the period of three years` THEN DATE_FROM_SERIAL ((DATE_SERIAL (`add years` `the first day of the period` 3)) MINUS 1)
    WHEN `s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) the period of 26 weeks` THEN DATE_FROM_SERIAL ((DATE_SERIAL `the first day of the period`) PLUS 181)

-- 7(6): the "disqualification period" in an offender's case means the relevant period beginning with a prescribed date
-- falling after the date of the conviction in the current set of proceedings.
-- src:2001 | (6) In subsection (6), for the words from “in relation to” to the end, there is substituted “ in an offender's case, means the relevant period beginning with a prescribed date falling after the date of the conviction in the current set of proceedings ”.
@ref s 119(6) SSFA 7(6) (src:2001-2001)
GIVEN `the date of the conviction in the current set of proceedings` IS A DATE  @desc On what date were you convicted in the current set of proceedings?
      `the prescribed date` IS A MAYBE DATE  @desc What is the prescribed date on which the disqualification period begins? Leave out if not known.
      `the relevant period` IS A `s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) relevant period`  @desc Which relevant period applies to you?
GIVETH A MAYBE Period
`s 119(6) SSFA 7(6) the disqualification period` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the prescribed date`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): the date is whatever regulations prescribe
    WHEN JUST begins THEN
        IF begins GREATER THAN `the date of the conviction in the current set of proceedings`
        THEN JUST (Period WITH `first day` IS begins, `last day` IS `s 119(7) SSFA 7(6A) the last day of the relevant period` `the relevant period` begins)
        ELSE NOTHING                                      -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): the period begins "with a prescribed date falling after the date of the conviction"

-- 7(7A): subsection (7B) applies where, after P's agreement to pay a penalty is taken into account for the purposes of
-- applying this section to P, (a) the agreement is withdrawn under subsection (5) of the appropriate penalty provision,
-- (b) it is decided on appeal or under regulations that the overpayment is not recoverable or due, or (c) the overpayment
-- is revised on appeal or under regulations and there is no new agreement by P to pay a penalty on the revised amount.
-- src:2007 | (7A) Subsection (7B) applies where, after the agreement of any person (“P”) to pay a penalty under the appropriate penalty provision is taken into account for the purposes of the application of this section in relation to that person—
-- src:2008 | (a) P's agreement to pay the penalty is withdrawn under subsection (5) of the appropriate penalty provision,
-- src:2009 | (b) it is decided on an appeal or in accordance with regulations under the Social Security Act 1998 or the Social Security (Northern Ireland) Order 1998 (S.I. 1998/1506 (N.I. 10)) that any overpayment to which the agreement relates is not recoverable or due, or
-- src:2010 | (c) the amount of any overpayment to which the penalty relates is revised on an appeal or in accordance with regulations under the Social Security Act 1998 or the Social Security (Northern Ireland) Order 1998 and there is no new agreement by P to pay a penalty under the appropriate penalty provision in relation to the revised overpayment.
@ref s 119(8) SSFA 7(7A) (src:2007-2010)
GIVEN `the agreement to pay the penalty has been taken into account in applying this section to the person` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has your agreement to pay the penalty been taken into account in applying this section to you?
      `the agreement is withdrawn under the appropriate penalty provision` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has your agreement to pay the penalty been withdrawn under subsection (5) of the penalty provision?
      `it is decided that the overpayment is not recoverable or due` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has it been decided on an appeal, or under social security regulations, that the overpayment is not recoverable or due?
      `the amount of the overpayment is revised on appeal or under regulations` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has the amount of the overpayment been revised on an appeal, or under social security regulations?
      `there is a new agreement to pay a penalty on the revised overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Have you agreed a new penalty on the revised overpayment?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 119(8) SSFA 7(7A) subsection (7B) applies` MEANS
    IF `the agreement to pay the penalty has been taken into account in applying this section to the person`
       AND (`the agreement is withdrawn under the appropriate penalty provision`
            OR `it is decided that the overpayment is not recoverable or due`
            OR (`the amount of the overpayment is revised on appeal or under regulations` AND (NOT (`there is a new agreement to pay a penalty on the revised overpayment`))))
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "Subsection (7B) applies where" is not "applies only where"

-- 7(7B): in those circumstances all payments and adjustments are made as would be necessary if no restriction had been
-- imposed that could not have been imposed if P had not agreed to pay the penalty.
-- src:2011 | (7B) In those circumstances, all such payments and other adjustments shall be made as would be necessary if no restriction had been imposed by or under this section that could not have been imposed if P had not agreed to pay the penalty.
@ref s 119(8) SSFA 7(7B) (src:2011-2011)
GIVEN `subsection (7B) applies` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Do the circumstances in section 7(7A) of the Fraud Act exist for you? Leave out if not known.
      `the restriction could have been imposed even if the person had not agreed to pay the penalty` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Could the restriction have been imposed on you even if you had not agreed to pay the penalty?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 119(8) SSFA 7(7B) the restriction is to be treated as not imposed` MEANS
    CONSIDER `subsection (7B) applies`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
        IF `the restriction could have been imposed even if the person had not agreed to pay the penalty`
        THEN JUST FALSE                                   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "as would be necessary if no restriction had been imposed ... that could not have been imposed if P had not agreed to pay the penalty" (this one could have been)
        ELSE JUST TRUE
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "In those circumstances"; outside them the subsection is silent
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (silent): whether the circumstances in (7A) exist is not known

-- 7(8): two defined terms, each "has the meaning given in" a section that is not among the sources.
-- src:2013 | “appropriate penalty provision” has the meaning given in section 6B(2)(a);”
@ref s 119(9) SSFA 7(8) “appropriate penalty provision” (src:2013-2013)
GIVEN `the provision is the one given in SSFA section 6B(2)(a)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Is the provision the one described in section 6B(2)(a) of the Fraud Act? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 119(9) SSFA 7(8) the provision is the appropriate penalty provision` MEANS
    `the provision is the one given in SSFA section 6B(2)(a)`  -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "has the meaning given in section 6B(2)(a)", which is not among the sources

-- src:2014 | “disqualifying event” has the meaning given in section 6B(13).
@ref s 119(9) SSFA 7(8) “disqualifying event” (src:2014-2014)
GIVEN `the event is the one given in SSFA section 6B(13)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Is the event one described in section 6B(13) of the Fraud Act? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 119(9) SSFA 7(8) the event is a disqualifying event` MEANS
    `the event is the one given in SSFA section 6B(13)`  -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "has the meaning given in section 6B(13)", which is not among the sources

-- 7(8A): where a person is convicted of more than one benefit offence in the same set of proceedings there is only one
-- disqualifying event for that set; (a) and (b) say when (1)(b) is satisfied and when the event is taken into account for (1)(d).
-- src:2016 | (8A) Where a person is convicted of more than one benefit offence in the same set of proceedings, there is to be only one disqualifying event in respect of that set of proceedings for the purposes of this section and—
@ref s 119(10) SSFA 7(8A) (src:2016-2016)
GIVEN `the number of benefit offences of which the person is convicted in the set of proceedings` IS A NUMBER  @desc Of how many benefit offences are you convicted in this set of proceedings?
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 119(10) SSFA 7(8A) the number of disqualifying events in the set of proceedings` MEANS
    IF `the number of benefit offences of which the person is convicted in the set of proceedings` GREATER THAN 1
    THEN JUST 1
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): the subsection applies "where a person is convicted of more than one benefit offence in the same set of proceedings"

-- (a): (1)(b) is satisfied if any of the convictions takes place in the five-year periods mentioned there.
-- src:2017 | (a) subsection (1)(b) is satisfied if any of the convictions take place in the five year periods mentioned there;
@ref s 119(10) SSFA 7(8A)(a) (src:2017-2017)
GIVEN `the dates on which the benefit offences were committed` IS A LIST OF DATE  @desc On what date was each benefit offence committed?
      `the dates on which the convictions in the earlier set of proceedings took place` IS A LIST OF DATE  @desc On what date did each conviction in the earlier set of proceedings take place?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 119(10) SSFA 7(8A)(a) subsection (1)(b) is satisfied` MEANS
    IF `s 119(5) SSFA 7(1)(b) a disqualifying event occurred within five years before a benefit offence` `the dates on which the benefit offences were committed` `the dates on which the convictions in the earlier set of proceedings took place`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "is satisfied if"; the paragraph does not say it is unsatisfied otherwise

-- (b): the event is taken into account for (1)(d) if any of the convictions has been taken into account as mentioned there.
-- src:2018 | (b) the event is taken into account for the purposes of subsection (1)(d) if any of the convictions have been taken into account as mentioned there;
@ref s 119(10) SSFA 7(8A)(b) (src:2018-2018)
GIVEN `whether each of the convictions has been taken into account for a previous application` IS A LIST OF BOOLEAN  @desc For each conviction in the earlier set of proceedings: has it been taken into account for an earlier application of these sections?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 119(10) SSFA 7(8A)(b) the event is taken into account for the purposes of subsection (1)(d)` MEANS
    IF any (GIVEN taken YIELD taken) `whether each of the convictions has been taken into account for a previous application`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "is taken into account ... if"; the paragraph does not say it is not otherwise

-- (c) and (d) are carried by the list inputs of 7(6A) above: the earlier disqualifying event counts on the date of any of its
-- convictions, and a previous disqualifying event is within the five years if any of its convictions is.
